{-
   Starting a thread, at the level where it is a machine operation.

   Beside `syscall0`..`syscall6` in spirit and in a file of its own because of what it is not: every
   one of those returns once, into the frame that made it. This one **returns twice** - once in the
   thread that called it, with the new thread's identifier, and once in the new thread, with a stack
   pointer of its own and the caller's every other register.

   That is why it cannot be written as `syscall5(clone, ...)` and a branch. A system call is emitted
   inline, so the code after it is the enclosing function's own - and in the new thread that code
   addresses locals at offsets into a stack nothing has written, through a frame pointer that still
   points into a frame belonging to a thread now running somewhere else. The new thread has to reach
   its entry function before it touches memory at all, out of registers and out of the stack the
   caller prepared for it. Every C library writes this one in assembly for the same reason.

   `Native.Thread` is what a program should use. This is the primitive under it, and using it
   directly means keeping every promise below.
-}

{-
   `cloneThread(flags, stackTop, entry, argument, threadId)` - the identifier, or a negated errno.

   What the caller owes it:

    - **`stackTop` is one past the highest byte of the new thread's stack, and is sixteen-byte
      aligned.** The thread grows downwards from there, and System V's alignment at a function's
      first instruction is derived from that address; an odd one gives a thread whose every spill is
      misaligned. Two words directly below it are used to hand the entry and the argument across and
      are not the thread's to keep.

    - **The storage lives at least as long as the thread does.** Nothing here owns it, nothing frees
      it, and the thread is still running when this returns.

    - **`entry` is an ordinary function of the language**, taking one pointer and returning nothing,
      and a closure is admitted as readily as a top-level one - both of its words cross to the new
      thread. It is called with `argument`, and when it returns the thread ends: `exit` and not
      `exit_group`, so the process carries on.

    - **`threadId` is a `%U32` the kernel clears when the thread ends**, and wakes a futex on, where
      the flags ask it to. It is what makes joining possible and is otherwise ignored.

    - **`flags` are the kernel's own**, and this knows none of them. `Native.Thread` states the set
      that makes a thread rather than a process.
-}
pub fn cloneThread(
  flags: Size, stackTop: %U8, entry: (%U8) -> {}, argument: %U8, threadId: %U32
) -> Size
